d  border crossings between ukraine and for  instance poland the neighboring  countries so uh the solution still needs  to be found to be honest how to deal  with this huge crisis looming  um the eu foreign ministers are also  meeting today in turin can you give us  an idea of what will be on the agenda  there  they will be meeting in a much broader  format we're talking here about the  council of europe and its members are  not only members of the european union  but also countries outside the block  like turkey for instance so ukraine is  of course uh front and center of the  agenda but i think this meeting is more  about exchanging views and proposals  uh on how to help ukraine  all right dw's alexander fundament in  brussels thank you  [Music]  miss orpilen you're attending a g7  development ministers meeting here in  berlin what is your main takeaway from  this we had very constructive a good  meeting and several  very timely topics we discussed on  i would say that the main message is  that  [Music]  even though  the ukrainian war or war in ukraine is  taking place in europe  the crisis is global  and that's why we cannot turn our back  to the rest of the world because we see  that the consequences of the war  are very very serious for instance in  the global south  are we turning the back on the global  south now  no we are not and that's very important  to tell also to our partners in the  global south  taking into account for instance food  security and food crisis which is a huge  challenge for instance in the uh horn of  africa or in the southern part of  sahel in  africa we are giving as the european  union and as a team europe so commission  and the member states over 1 billion  euros mainly humanitarian assistance to  those regions so that they can really  buy more food and they are able to feed  their citizens  but that's not the only uh action we are  taking we are also investing in the food  production capacities in africa  because africa imports 80